{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1021", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "PARAGRAPH 1:\nTHE GIVEN STATEMENT PRESENTS AN OVERLY SIMPLISTIC AND POTENTIALLY DANGEROUS PHILOSOPHY ON CIVIC OBLIGATION AND LEGAL COMPLIANCE. BY SUGGESTING THAT INDIVIDUALS MAKE SUBJECTIVE JUDGMENTS ABOUT WHETHER A LAW IS \"BAD\" AND THEN DEFY IT, THE SENTENCE IGNORES THE COMPLEXITY OF LEGAL SYSTEMS, INSTITUTIONAL PROCESSES FOR REFORM, AND THE CONSEQUENCES OF ANARCHIC LEGAL DISOBEDIENCE. THIS APPROACH ALSO LACKS CLARITY ON WHO DECIDES WHAT CONSTITUTES A \"BAD\" LAW—AN INDIVIDUAL’S PERSONAL MORALITY OR SOME OTHER STANDARD—WHICH COULD LEAD TO ARBITRARY INTERPRETATIONS AND SOCIAL CHAOS.\n\nPARAGRAPH 2:\nFURTHERMORE, THE STATEMENT FAILS TO ACCOUNT FOR THE ROLE OF LAW AS A MECHANISM FOR ORDER, PROTECTION OF RIGHTS, AND RESOLUTION OF CONFLICTS. EVEN WELL-INTERNEDTIONED LAWS REQUIRE COLLECTIVE ADHERENCE TO FUNCTION; MASS DISREGARD FOR LEGAL NORMS, JUSTIFIED BY SUBJECTIVE MORALITY, ERODES TRUST IN INSTITUTIONS AND UNDERMINES THE RULE OF LAW ITSELF. A MORE PRODUCTIVE FRAMEWORK WOULD EMPHASIZE ENGAGING WITH LEGAL SYSTEMS THROUGH PROPER CHANNELS—SUCH AS ADVOCACY, LITIGATION, OR DEMOCRATIC REFORM—RATHER THAN UNILATERAL REJECTION OF LAWS.", "logprobs": [{"token": "PAR", "logprob": -0.1297348290681839}, {"token": "AG", "logprob": -0.00016223068814724684}, {"token": "RAP", "logprob": -0.009271660819649696}, {"token": "H", "logprob": -8.260862523457035e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.04448649659752846}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00041762212640605867}, {"token": ":\n", "logprob": -0.3010086119174957}, {"token": "THE", "logprob": -0.12522362172603607}, {"token": " G", "logprob": -3.0102758407592773}, {"token": "IV", "logprob": -0.0012677022023126483}, {"token": "EN", "logprob": -0.0019646643195301294}, {"token": " STATE", "logprob": -0.32154932618141174}, {"token": "MENT", "logprob": -4.6967357775429264e-05}, {"token": " PRE", "logprob": -0.6482993960